# Coding Practices

This guide outlines the essential rules for developing high-quality Vue 3 applications. Adhere to these standards to ensure consistency, readability, and long-term maintainability across our codebase.

## 1. Code Organization

- **Single responsibility**: Each source file should have a clear, focused scope/purpose
- **Split large files**: Break files when they become large or handle too many concerns
- **Constants extraction**: Move constants to a dedicated `[page dir]/constants/*.[js|ts]` or `[component dir]/constants.[js|ts]` file



## 2. Foundational Setup: Linting & Formatting

**Always use ESLint and Stylelint.** This combination enforces consistent code style and catches common errors automatically, streamlining development and code reviews. Integrate them into your IDE for real-time feedback and format-on-save.

## 3. Single-File Components (SFCs)

Each component **must** reside in its own `.vue` file.

❌ BAD
```js
// app.component('TodoList', { /* ... */ })
// app.component('TodoItem', { /* ... */ })
```

✅ GOOD
```
components/
├── TodoList.vue
└── TodoItem.vue
```

## 4. Filename Case Style

Use **kebab-case** for all filenames.

❌ BADs
```
Components/
├── MyComponent.vue
└── myComponent.vue
```

✅ GOOD
```
components/
└── my-component.vue
```

## 5. CSS Code Style

- If there are global variables（from `@deot/style`）, then use them first.
- Prioritize the use of the `BEM` standard.

## 6. Javascript/TypeScript Code Style

- Use arrow functions

## 7. Response Language

Always respond in Chinese-simplified
